Environmental Activists Demand Australia Stop Sending Plastic Waste To Indonesia

SURABAYA, INDONESIA - AUGUST 06: Indonesia environmental activists hold a protest demanding Australia stops sending plastic waste to Indonesia featuring large sculptures depicting a baby and a pregnant woman contaminated with plastic waste in front of the Australia Consulate General on August 06, 2025 in Surabaya, Indonesia. Ecological Observation and Wetland Conservation (ECOTON) reported that, between 2020 and 2024, Australia was the largest exporter of plastic waste to Indonesia, sending a total of 2.7 billion kilograms according to the UN Comtrade Database (2024). They found that 60–82% of Australian paper waste sent to Indonesia was contaminated with flexible plastic scrap, multilayer plastics, and soiled plastic packaging — materials that cannot be recycled and instead accumulate in illegal dumpsites. Between September and November 2024, ECOTON tested 83 baby care products and found that 58% contained microbeads — small plastic particles that can enter the body through the skin. Research indicates that microplastics may cause systemic inflammation, interfere with blood clotting, cause arrhythmias, trigger heart cell death (apoptosis), promote tissue fibrosis, and increase the risks of heart failure and stroke. Babies are especially vulnerable, potentially ingesting up to 660,000 microplastic particles per year, highlighting the dangers these substances pose to both the environment and infant health. (Photo by Robertus Pudyanto/Getty Images)

